-Applying a supported migration — idempotent phases, read-back, a receipt with before
-and after hashes — is not implemented. Neither is rollback, and the issue says
-plainly that keeping old blobs does not by itself provide it.
+### Applying one
+
+A migration is applied in three phases that may be cut short at any point and
+resumed by asking again.
+
+**The receipt is the anchor.** Its identifier is derived from the plan — source,
+target, workflow and the exact task set — so a second run finds the open receipt and
+continues it rather than starting a rival. Nothing mints an identifier the caller
+could get wrong, and a record whose id does not describe its own contents is refused
+on read: it describes a different migration than the one it claims to be.
+
+**Both halves of the pin move.** A pin carries a distribution digest and a declared
+shape, and admission refuses on either, so moving only the digest would seal a
+receipt while leaving the task parked — the operation claiming success and achieving
+nothing, with a read-back that checks the same half the move wrote and therefore
+cannot see it. The receipt records the target shape too: one that cannot say what it
+migrated onto is not a receipt.
+
+**Move, then read back, then seal.** A task already on the target is skipped, which
+is what makes a resumed run safe. A task on some third digest stops everything —
+something else moved it, and continuing on a plan that no longer describes reality is
+how a receipt ends up asserting something untrue. Only after every task has been
+re-read from the store and found on the target is `completed_at` written, so an
+interruption anywhere leaves the receipt open and the next run finishes it.
+
+The verification returns the receipt the sealing step needs, so skipping it does not
+merely fail a test — it stops compiling. That was deliberate: no test can catch its
+removal from inside a single call, because the read-back can only fail if a write did
+not stick, and a rule that depends on remembering it is not a rule.
+
+What this moves is the whole pin — both halves — and not the code: the
+distribution's bytes are still not stored, so a migrated task runs on whatever the
+registry serves. Rollback is not
+implemented, and the issue says plainly that keeping old blobs does not by itself
+provide it.
 
